            <ArticleTitle>[Peritoneal tuberculosis in children: report of two cases].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>822-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="UNLABELLED">Peritoneal tuberculosis is an uncommon presentation of extra-pulmonary tuberculosis in children. It usually presents as ascites, abdominal pain, anorexia and weight loss.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASES REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">We report two adolescent patients who presented with ascites, fever, weight loss and abdominal distension. In one case, the diagnosis was late, and confirmed by ascites culture. In the second case, a laparoscopy was performed and showed whitish nodules involving the entire abdominal cavity, compatible with peritoneal tuberculosis, later confirmed bacteriologically.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Peritoneal tuberculosis presents with nonspecific symptoms. Because laboratory investigations may not be helpful, diagnosis may be difficult. Peritoneal-fluid adenosine deaminase (ADA) determination and coelioscopy seem to be the best way to make a rapid diagnosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
